{% /table %}

## Circle of Willis and compensation

In the circle of Willis, the blood in the three communicating arteries is normally static. If one of the large arteries feeding the circle is blocked, the other two can compensate more or less completely through the communicating arteries. If the basilar artery is occluded, each posterior cerebral artery may be supplied by the internal carotid artery of its own side.

## Mnemonics

{% callout type="tip" %}
**Direction of crossed signs:** cranial nerves stay on the same side as the lesion. The body is on the opposite side. Face same, body opposite.
{% /callout %}

## Practice MCQs

{% mcq answer="C" explanation="The pons lies between the midbrain above and the medulla below. The rule of 4 places CN V, VI, VII and VIII in the pons." %}
Which group of cranial nerves is associated mainly with the pons?
- A. III, IV, V and VI
- B. VI, VII, VIII and IX
- C. V, VI, VII and VIII
- D. IX, X, XI and XII
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="B" explanation="Trochlear nerve (CN IV) is the only cranial nerve that exits from the back of the brain stem, just below the inferior colliculus." %}
Which cranial nerve emerges from the dorsal surface of the brain stem?
- A. Oculomotor
- B. Trochlear
- C. Abducens
- D. Hypoglossal
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="D" explanation="The superior cerebellar peduncle connects the cerebellum to the upper pons on the way to the midbrain and thalamus. The inferior peduncle goes to the medulla and the middle peduncle to the pons." %}
The superior cerebellar peduncle connects the cerebellum to which part of the brain stem?
- A. Medulla
- B. Lower pons
- C. Spinal cord
- D. Upper pons, en route to the midbrain
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="A" explanation="Corticospinal fibres have not yet crossed in the pons, so the arm and leg weakness is on the opposite side. Cranial nerves project to the same side, so CN VII weakness is on the side of the lesion." %}
A lesion of the left pons is most likely to cause:
- A. Left facial weakness with right arm and leg weakness
- B. Right facial weakness with right arm and leg weakness
- C. Left facial weakness with left arm and leg weakness
- D. Right facial weakness with left arm and leg weakness
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="B" explanation="The posterior inferior cerebellar artery usually arises from the vertebral artery and supplies the medulla. The SCA and AICA usually arise from the basilar artery." %}
The posterior inferior cerebellar artery usually arises from which artery?
- A. Basilar
- B. Vertebral
- C. Posterior cerebral
- D. Internal carotid
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="C" explanation="Pontine reticular nuclei excite the antigravity muscles through the pontine reticulospinal tract. The medullary reticular nuclei relax the same muscles, so the two sets work against each other." %}
Which statement about the reticular nuclei is correct?
- A. Medullary nuclei excite the antigravity muscles
- B. Pontine nuclei relax the antigravity muscles
- C. Pontine nuclei excite and medullary nuclei relax the antigravity muscles
- D. Both sets excite the antigravity muscles
{% /mcq %}

## Viva questions

{% viva question="What are the parts of the brain stem and where does it lie?" %}
The medulla oblongata, pons and midbrain. It lies in the posterior cranial fossa with its ventral surface on the clivus.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="State the rule of 4 of the brain stem." %}
There are 4 medial structures beginning with M (corticospinal tract, medial lemniscus, medial longitudinal fasciculus, motor nuclei and nerves) and 4 side structures beginning with S (spinocerebellar tracts, spinothalamic tract, sensory nucleus of CN V, sympathetics). There are 4 cranial nerves in the medulla (IX to XII) and 4 in the pons (V to VIII), and the rest are above the pons.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Which cranial nerve exits posteriorly from the brain stem?" %}
The trochlear nerve (CN IV), just below the inferior colliculus.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Name the three cerebellar peduncles and what they connect." %}
The inferior peduncle connects the medulla to the cerebellum, the middle peduncle connects the pons to the cerebellum, and the superior peduncle connects the cerebellum to the upper pons on the way to the midbrain and thalamus.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Describe the blood supply of the brain stem." %}
The midbrain is supplied by the posterior cerebral, superior cerebellar and basilar arteries. The pons is supplied by pontine branches of the basilar artery and the AICA. The medulla is supplied by medullary branches of the vertebral artery and the PICA, and the anterior spinal artery supplies the medial medulla.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="What are crossed signs?" %}
Ipsilateral cranial nerve or facial signs with contralateral weakness or sensory loss in the body, caused by a one-sided brain stem lesion. They occur because cranial nerves project to the same side, while the long tracts have not yet crossed.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="What does the reticular formation do?" %}
It regulates vital functions such as cardiac and respiratory activity, keeps the cortex aroused so that we stay conscious, and helps regulate muscle tone, posture and reflexes.
{% /viva %}

